# Line endings are not cosmetic in this repo.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` (the Windows default) rewrites text files to CRLF on
# checkout. For a POSIX shell script that is fatal in a quiet way: the kernel
# hands `#!/usr/bin/env sh` a file whose first line is `sh\r`, and every line
# ends in `\r`, so `sh scripts/cfpress.sh` dies with
# `\r: command not found` or, worse, runs with mangled arguments.
#
# `.ps1` needs the opposite guarantee for a different reason: Windows
# PowerShell 5.1 reads a BOM-less file as ANSI/GBK, which turns every Chinese
# label in the menu into mojibake *at parse time*. The BOM is a byte, and a
# checkout that "normalises" the file is one way to lose it. `working-tree-encoding`
# keeps the BYTES pinned rather than the line endings.
#
# So: pin each extension to the form its interpreter actually requires, instead
# of letting the developer's autocrlf setting decide.

# --- shell: always LF, in the repo and in the working tree -------------------
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf
*.mjs           text eol=lf
*.cjs           text eol=lf
*.js            text eol=lf
*.ts            text eol=lf
*.tsx           text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.jsonc         text eol=lf
*.sql           text eol=lf
*.css           text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
# Theme templates. The engine tolerates CRLF (verified: a CRLF template renders
# identical output), so this is not a correctness pin — it is a *diff* pin. With
# `core.autocrlf=true`, an unpinned `.html` shows as modified after every
# checkout, and a reviewer then has to re-derive that the change is whitespace.
# Same reason as the shell scripts: the form is decided here, not by the
# developer's git config.
*.html          text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf

# --- PowerShell: keep CRLF; the BOM is the writer's job ---------------------
# `working-tree-encoding=UTF-8-BOM` would be the elegant way to pin the BOM,
# but it needs an `iconv` that knows that encoding — and Git for Windows ships
# one that does not (`iconv -f UTF-8-BOM` → "conversion from UTF-8-BOM
# unsupported"), which makes `git add` fail outright with
#
#     error: failed to encode 'scripts/cfpress.ps1' from UTF-8-BOM to UTF-8
#
# An attribute that breaks `git add` on the platform the file is written for is
# worse than no attribute. So the BOM is asserted, not transported:
# `tests/launcher-parity.test.mjs` reads the raw bytes of scripts/cfpress.ps1
# and fails if the first three are not EF BB BF.
#
# CRLF is still pinned, because that one git can do and it matters: PS 5.1 is
# happy either way, but a stray LF-only file confuses some Windows editors into
# rewriting the whole file (and dropping the BOM while they are at it).
*.ps1           text eol=crlf
*.psd1          text eol=crlf
*.psm1          text eol=crlf

# --- binaries: leave alone --------------------------------------------------
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.webp          binary
*.ico           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
*.zip           binary
*.sqlite        binary
